I'm P Burgess and I'm the lead Foley  artist at Pinewood Studios we always  describe movie magic and they are the  secret life of movies while there is a  huge secret world behind the screen  obviously and Foley is one of them  basically my job is to recreate sound  effects that have happened in a film  very few sounds are fully recorded on  set and so there's a post-production  device put into place to help create  more sense to go on the film we perform  them we physically manipulate props in  order to make this end much more organic  rather than a track laid sound like a  door closing offered CD library or of  the Internet  it's like mimetics you have to you have  to kind of recreate the characters that  are on the screen and put them into your  performance often the original  recordings aren't very clear for the  spot effects which are the props the  things that the actors are picking up  and using or the footsteps like this it  could be a set so it's supposed to look  like a marble floor but it's just wood  so we'll then perform the footsteps  again or marble and make it sound like a  massive beautiful hole that's supposed  to be so as you can see we've got lots  of different surfaces I'm currently on  the concrete so we can add dust to this  and make it more gritty or we could add  we do the snow on here as well so we've  got a big sort of curtain load full of  industrial salt so we'll put that on  here we've got the earth pit here which  we can turn into marshes we turn into  the jungle loads of different things and  that all depends on what plants we put  in there so I've see at the moment we're  doing grass because we have grass in  there  and just behind it we've got the river  pit so we fill that up with water and we  would do the ocean or rivers or any sort  of water  and for Tarzan we actually filled it up  because they spend a lot of time running  in and out of the rivers in the jungle  so he put sort of a mud Bank on one side  of it so he could run down it and into  the water and then be absolutely covered  in mud a job will come in and we will  speak to the sound supervisor or the  post-production coordinator as soon as  there's a picture for us to work with  we'll get a copy of it and we'll begin  prepping the the shoot itself so someone  or many people in my team will go  through and plot out in the proto-tool  session every footstep that happens in  that whole film every cloth Russell  jewelry jangle every door that closes  hand down everything is planned so it  becomes just a huge map of sound doesn't  necessarily mean we'll shoot all of that  but it means that I know it's all being  identified and it's all there on the  screen and then as we begin our brief  what the team here is what we're doing  what the style approaches what you know  how we're going to approach this  particular film maybe only 10% or  sometimes as much as 60% of what we do  in this studio will be heard within the  final mix but it's about giving the  mixers as much ammunition sound wise as  possible to make a really buried  interesting soundtrack that does drive  the story so when recording Foley we  will break it into three little sections  feet moves and spots feet are pretty  self-explanatory the Foley artists will  select a shoe that matches that of the  characters and walk in time to the  character moving on the screen moves is  their cloth moves because actually  watching a film without any of that is  quite weird so they'll get a coat or a  bit of fabric that matches what the  character is wearing on screen  and just move in time with the  characters actions and spot effects are  anything that the actors or anything  interesting that happens on-screen that  obviously need to sound so a car door  closing Keys going into ignitions or  juggling anything any literally anything  that happens on screen we will try and  recreate so props generally come from  all over the place we all end up going  around second-hand shops and if there's  something that you pick up and it's got  a nice noise you might just buy it  anyway it's not so much the the object  as the the noise it makes and that's how  we we look at props just on the table  here if I reach over there's a perfect  example and although it does look like  the substance you would use it for this  is just a pillowcase full of corn flour  but it it has an amazing crushing sound  that we use for snow so if you add this  to a layer of snow you get that fresh  crushing kind of saying so that's one  example you just spend your life picking  things up and listening to them and  imagining what it could possibly be and  and now I have I have a an amazing sound  library in my head for what noise  objects make and how to manipulate them  it's really exciting when folio us we'll  go and find a prop and come out and  recreate exactly how you imagined  something to sound I find that really  exciting  [Music]  okay well please come in this is part of  my props tour this is the metal room we  need so many different props when we're  working on a film though I have to have  lots and lots of choices I try to keep  them in order otherwise they end up in  great big piles everywhere  lovely weapons against the wall here I  need several choices of weapons because  we don't want to keep the same you know  sword swinging in every every film we've  got a little costume department over  here for lots of different fabrics so we  can do moves and if there's a military  person we've got military uniforms we've  got trays as well so we use them as  shields for instance or just as trays  lovely resonating metal boxes because  there's so many times when for instance  if you're on a submarine we can't really  build up the space of a submarine and  you can hear my voice already resonating  against the metal oh no whoever the  submarine so we we would put that next  to the microphone and it would pick up  my movements and my boots and give it a  little of a mental resonance this is  scaffold stuff then we can make little  ratalie scaffolding noises we often use  those to add guns and things should we  go to the other room which is more of a  generic kind of prop as you can see it's  slightly different to the metal room in  as much as this is everything else that  isn't much you've already seen shoes  next door but we've got a much bigger  collection than the ones that are  actually in the theater again for choice  more than anything else  lots of boxes filled with shoes and  boots as we move along we kind of just  get more into communication books  telephones computers radios lovely  arrive hats along the top these are me  home exactly  noted by Band of Brothers moving along  we get a bit more generic into travel so  things like gloves wallets purses things  personal things that people would have  on them again you can imagine I don't  use the same players every time somebody  gets money out of the post I've got  about 50 purses 50 wallets and we just  mix it up all the time more of the  lovely Zoe my assistant shoes which she  has in here these they particularly like  ripped cloths things I can just tear up  and not ruin the cloth with but just  whip them for a way like I said bedding  for wigs  I've got lots of different types of wigs  and fair and things to make creature  noises broken china and broken tiles and  things because we don't always like to  buy absolutely brand-new and then break  it and because we're just making a smash  sound you can have actually have the  bits to begin with and then drop them  and it still makes the sound of a solid  object breaking little jewelry section  over here with a lovely vintage tail  which we adore and just about everything  in here is used for multiple purposes  everything we do is bespoke nothing is  repeated over and over again it would be  for me would be considered very lazy if  we did that kind of thing when we have  so many different choices to do things  from and the way I work as an artist is  to be as creative you know each day as a  few favorite projects of mine that we've  gotten to work on every year's one of  them that really stands out is ex  machina everything within that was  really interesting for being in the  theater to witness how it was recorded  because pretty much the whole building  is glass so what we did was would put up  all these big panes of glass around vast  Pete was performing some of the Foley  for it like any of the doors and the  footsteps in places it was all really  interesting to see and I think in the  end because there's only three people in  the whole of the film pretty much I  think it's really open to Foley and  stuff like that so I really enjoyed  witnessing one of that happen some of  the projects that I've worked on have  been transporting to Wonderwoman King  Arthur baby driver  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  all the principals are working to sync  with the right shoes and doing all the  rest of the things was completely thrown  out the window a baby driver because  every pair of shoes needed to sit in not  necessarily always into the world but  actually into the music sequence it  depended whether it's real world or  music we actually were wearing  headphones to make sure that we were  doing that and then altogether we'd  listen back and go actually the coffee  cups are slightly too hard or the shoes  aren't quite right because they're  poking through in slightly the wrong  frequency P and Zoe and Jason  particularly needed to pull themselves  away from thing can actually be  listening to the music and trying to  walk or run as close to the sink without  stepping outside of the music so we  don't like change shoes or Glenn and  Gemma would change something on the desk  because you don't want to interrupt the  music you just want to be there with it  [Music]  we were always thinking well is this  actually going to work with the music so  as a result we ended up using all  different things that we normally  wouldn't use in that film because it  complemented the music more and filled  in with the Sam supervisors sort of  vision for this job so that was we were  quite proud of the way that came out  like I said that was a very big sound  team collaborative effort with you know  big free last team and then I was  helping out it really does vary  depending on the project and depending  on how much post-production budget the  project has as to how long we can  actually spend in theatre for me  physically to perform up to perform a  film comfortably let's say a kitchen  sink drama that doesn't have a great  deal of CGI and it's no say in the war  it's a generic soundtrack eight days  would be comfortable for me four days  doing the feet maybe a day doing the  moves and then three or four days doing  the sound effects on a bigger project  with bigger budgets will tend to get a  longer time and maybe two weeks three  weeks on the final Harry Potter movies  there was five weeks there was so much  content in those films we might have  shot all of this media do we need all  this media maybe we don't actually maybe  we're out these ten sets of footsteps  maybe we only need three of them because  actually we're trying to pull focus on  the narrative that is happening over  here  so we don't a clutter the scene with  everything else because that could draw  attention or we just do just don't need  that sounds like actually it's actually  nicer to be simpler so we'll start  making those calls  editorially like creatively as we go  along generally in Foley we with notes  all without notes part of my joy is to  try and recreate everything whether it's  somebody's already sound designed a  dragon if I've got the time I might just  have a go anyway and offer up you know  something that they might not have  thought about all an addition to the  sound and then it's up to us it's up to  somebody else to decide by the  I don't know it's all really fun  everything about it I really enjoy doing  something that people might think is  quite mundane up to doing things like  car crashes and explosions I like  finding all the little details pete has  definitely been one of my biggest  inspirations like coming to work every  day with someone who absolutely lost a  job and is willing to help you with  anything is incredible I've had the best  tutor but also the whole team like  everybody is so passionate and driven  about what we do that we're all working  together it's like yeah we've got this  and we can do it and look at what we've  just made isn't it amazing  that's great I think the things I enjoy  most is is just that instant creativity  that we that we we have to employ I mean  there is no training course that you can  go to and do for Foley's it's really  about self-expression and about building  of a library within your head and a  dexterity with objects so it's it's it's  just a very interesting crazy kind of  kind of job  [Music]

